  146: ## Decisions
  147: 
  148: ### A. The tenant-read rule ships as ONE module, and the acceptance suite drives it
  149: 
  150: `supabase/functions/_shared/visibility.ts`, under the same two constraints `memberships.ts` states
  151: of itself: zero non-relative imports, no Deno global, no I/O, no clock. It is compiled by
  152: `tests/at/tsconfig.json` (strict, `skipLibCheck: false`, no DOM) and run by Deno in the edge
  153: runtime.
  154: 
  155: It holds exactly three things:
  156: 
  157: - `tenantReadAllowed(viewer, scope)` - the one rule. An NGO account reads an organisation's
  158:   non-public data only where it holds a membership row IN THAT organisation. A volunteer reads a
  159:   project's non-public data only where it is THAT project's assigned volunteer. A platform admin
  160:   reads both, for every account. A logged-out viewer reads neither.
  161: - `publicProjectView(project, organization)` - what a project shows the world. One statement of
  162:   what is public, so "public" is a value this repository can point at rather than a habit.
  163: - `TENANT_NOT_FOUND` - see decision B.
  164: 
  165: It fails closed on every value it does not recognise, the posture `parseOrgRole` states and gives
  166: its reasons for. The decision is the shared module's; the edge functions consult it; the loop-tier
  167: adapter delegates to it, so a loop green is a claim about shipped code.
  168: 
  169: **Why the argument shape matters.** `tenantReadAllowed` takes the viewer's membership IN THE TARGET
  170: organisation, never a list of the viewer's memberships. An implementation that authorised from the
  171: caller's standing in some other organisation cannot express itself through the signature at all -
  172: the same device `orgAdminActionAllowed` uses, and the same reason.
  173: 
  174: ### B. "No existence oracle" becomes STRUCTURAL, not a rule somebody applies
  175: 
  176: The criterion's clause is that a denial must not reveal whether the thing exists. That is a
  177: property of two answers being IDENTICAL, so the module exports the answer itself:
  178: 
  179: ```
  180: TENANT_NOT_FOUND = { status: 404, body: { ok: false, reason: '<one sentence>' } }
  181: ```
  182: 
  183: Every non-public read surface returns exactly this constant for BOTH "no such row" and "exists,
  184: not yours". There is nowhere in the surface to put a second refusal, so the two cannot drift apart
  185: by an edit that looks harmless. The test compares the two responses byte for byte and asserts the
  186: same status, not merely that both refused.
  187: 
  188: **404 rather than 403, and the alternative was considered.** `update-organization` uses one 403 for
  189: both cases and is equally non-oracular; either works so long as it is the SAME. 404 is chosen
  190: because it carries no information at all, while a 403 states that something is there to be
  191: forbidden. The cost is that a 404 for a row that does exist is, strictly, not true - and that cost
  192: is the point of the criterion.
  193: 
  194: **What is NOT claimed: timing.** Response time is a side channel this item does not measure and
  195: does not defend. The merge ruling states it.
  196: 
  197: **The outage case is neither.** A read that FAILED - the database unreachable - answers 502 and
  198: says so, the shape `update-organization` lines 101-106 already use, so an outage can never be read
  199: by a test as the isolation property holding.
  200: 
  201: ### C. Enforcement lands in the database, not only in the function
  202: 
  203: The migration adds the policy set. Two stable `security definer` helpers keyed on `auth.uid()` -
  204: `public.viewer_is_org_member(uuid)` and `public.viewer_is_platform_admin()` - so a policy on
  205: `org_memberships` does not recurse into itself. Then `select` policies on `organizations`,
  206: `org_memberships`, `acknowledgments` and `projects`, and `grant select ... to authenticated` on
  207: each. **Nothing is granted to `anon`**: the public surface is an edge function, not a table.
  208: 
  209: **Why both layers.** The Data API is literally the criterion's "direct API/ID probing", and with a
  210: policy a denied keyed read returns `[]` - the same answer as a row that does not exist, so the
  211: no-oracle property is free there. The edge function is the path a UI must use (`CLAUDE.md`: the UI
  212: never touches the database directly) and it needs decision B to get the same property. A green on
  213: one arm alone would leave the other open.
  214: 
  215: ### D. Three read surfaces, each traceable to a named clause
  216: 
  217: | function | `verify_jwt` | clause it serves |
  218: |---|---|---|
  219: | `organization-dashboard` | true | AT-001.21 "NGO A's non-public data ... dashboard" |
  220: | `project-workspace` | true | AT-001.22 "that project's non-public data", AT-001.23 "that project's working data" |
  221: | `public-project-page` | **false** | AT-001.22 "the public project page remains visible", AT-001.24 "public surfaces (listings, project pages)" |
  222: 
  223: `public-project-page` is the first `verify_jwt = false` block in this repository and the config
  224: comment that says all functions are authenticated is corrected in the same change. It reveals that
  225: a project exists, deliberately: that is the criterion's own "beyond public surfaces" carve-out, and
  226: keeping it a separate function is what stops the public answer contaminating the no-oracle test.
  227: 
  228: None of the three is a general reader. Each returns one projection, the way
  229: `update-organization` refuses to be a general profile editor.
  230: 
  231: ### E. What this green covers, said before it is built
  232: 
  233: The criteria enumerate drafts, ledger, files, thread, tasks and dashboard. **Only the dashboard
  234: kind exists** - an organisation, its projects, its membership and its acknowledgments. The others
  235: are surfaces no requirement has landed. This item isolates every kind of tenant data that exists
  236: and states the rest as absent, in the plan, in the per-id table and in the merge ruling.
  237: 
  238: **And it makes the absence self-correcting.** A conformance arm at integration tier reads the live
  239: catalog and fails when a table in `public` is neither declared as unreachable by any client role
  240: nor covered by a select policy. The declared list is shipped code, so a table added by a later
  241: requirement fails the build until somebody decides which it is. This is the device the manifest
  242: already ratifies for D6.L2's write routes, applied to reads. Without it this leaf's green would be
  243: a statement about today's four tables and nothing else.
  244: 
  245: ### F. AT-001.24's UI clause cannot be built here - the decision lands, the screens do not
  246: 
  247: The criterion is about a browser: public surfaces render, authenticated surfaces redirect to
  248: sign-in. `src/` is Lovable territory and CI fails a pull request that touches both territories
  249: (fact 6). There are also no screens to guard (fact 5).
  250: 
  251: So this item lands the three halves it can:
  252: 
  253: 1. the logged-out denial on every non-public API surface - 401 from both authenticated functions,
  254:    and a privilege refusal from the Data API, both measured;
  255: 2. the public surface answering an anonymous caller with the public projection and nothing else;
  256: 3. a shipped route registry naming each route under `src/routes/` as public or authenticated, with
  257:    a conformance arm that fails when a route is not declared. It reads `src/routes/` and
  258:    `src/routeTree.gen.ts` and writes neither - the same out-of-band shape `_source-scan.ts` uses
  259:    for AT-001.17.
  260: 
  261: At integration tier AT-001.24 REFUSES with `CapabilityPending(['ui.logged-out-surface-rendering'])`
  262: - the shape AT-001.05 and AT-001.10 already use. A green there would claim a rendering nobody
  263: observed.
  264: 
  265: **This is the open question for the founder. See the last section.**
  266: 
  267: ### G. Every denial body carries a positive control
  268: 
  269: A surface that refused everybody would satisfy AT-001.21, .22 and .24 completely while proving
  270: nothing. Every body therefore performs the ALLOWED read first and asserts it succeeded, so each
  271: refusal is attributable to the tenant boundary rather than to the surface being shut. This is the
  272: discipline every landed body in this suite already applies.
  273: 
  274: ### H. Givens come from the operator, and the plan says so
  275: 
  276: No product path creates a project or assigns a volunteer (`_contract.ts` says so of
  277: `createProjectAsOperator` and `assignVolunteerAsOperator`). The two-organisation Given uses
  278: `createOrganizationAsOperator` and `grantMembershipAsOperator`, which exist. The platform admin
  279: uses `provisionPlatformAdmin`, which exists. **No new operator authority is invented** - every
  280: Given this item needs is already on the surface. Only READ members are added.
  281: 
  282: ---
  283: 
