  293: /* ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ the SUT */
  294: 
  295: /**
  296:  * REQ-001's accounts system under test.
  297:  *
  298:  * The split is the point, and there are now FOUR kinds of member here rather than three:
  299:  *
  300:  *   1. THE TWO PRODUCT OPERATIONS â€” `completeSignup` and `createOrganization`. At loop tier the
  301:  *      adapter runs them over its own storage but delegates every judgement to
  302:  *      `supabase/functions/_shared/accounts.ts`, the module the edge functions import; at
  303:  *      integration tier they would be the deployed edge functions.
  304:  *   2. SUPABASE AUTH'S HALF â€” registration, sign-in, identity linking, the session layer
  305:  *      (`signOut`, `refreshSession`, `sessionsOf`), password reset (`requestPasswordReset`,
  306:  *      `emailedPasswordResetLink`, `completePasswordReset`) and the verification state
  307:  *      (`emailVerified`, `emailedVerificationLink`, `useVerificationLink`). None of it is this
  308:  *      requirement's code. It is what produces the states the product operations judge.
  309:  *
  310:  *      THE SESSION AND RESET MEMBERS ARE VENDOR MIRRORS, and every one of them is named in
  311:  *      `_fixture.ts`'s mirror section with what binds it live or an explicit unbound label. What is
  312:  *      NOT a mirror is the judgement that reads them: every session-taking operation resolves its
  313:  *      caller through the SHIPPED `callerFromAuthAnswer`, so a dead session is refused by the same
  314:  *      code the deployed functions run.
  315:  *   3. READ-BACK â€” the row shapes an assertion reads after the fact.
  316:  *   4. ONE STAND-IN SURFACE, AND IT IS THE ONLY ONE â€” `sendDiscoveryMessage`, with
  317:  *      `discoveryMessagesBy` reading it back.
  318:  *
  319:  * THE FOURTH KIND NEEDS ITS OWN SENTENCE, because it is the one that could be misread. NO
  320:  * DISCOVERY ROUTE EXISTS IN THIS TREE. The Discovery message route is REQ-002/004's, and what
  321:  * this requirement's leaf ships is the DECISION that route must consult â€”
  322:  * `discoveryMessageAllowed` in `supabase/functions/_shared/verification.ts`. So
  323:  * `sendDiscoveryMessage` is not a deployed operation being tested; it is a stand-in surface whose
  324:  * only job is to put the shipped decision on a tested path, exactly as
  325:  * `hasPlatformAcknowledgment` below is the hook a project-creation leaf must call. A green over
  326:  * it says the DECISION is right. It says nothing about enforcement anywhere, because there is
  327:  * nowhere yet to enforce it.
  328:  */
  329: export type AccountsSut = {
  330:   /** AT-001.07's second clause: which types the PUBLIC signup surface offers. */
  331:   publicSignupAccountTypes(): Promise<readonly string[]>;
  332: 
  333:   /* --- Supabase Auth's half. Not this leaf's code; it is what produces a session to complete. --- */
  334: 
  335:   /**
  336:    * Register an auth user with an email and a password, and return the resulting session.
  337:    *
  338:    * ============================================================================================
  339:    * IT STILL RETURNS A SESSION, AND THE LIVE STACK ISSUES NONE. THIS IS THE DECLARED DIVERGENCE.
  340:    * ============================================================================================
  341:    *
  342:    * With `enable_confirmations = true` the live GoTrue issues NO session at signup: it creates the
  343:    * user, sends the confirmation email, and answers with no tokens. This fixture mints one anyway,
  344:    * because every body written before this leaf takes its session from here and rewriting them all
  345:    * would be a change to nine green ids for no criterion's sake.
  346:    *
  347:    * SO REGISTRATION ISSUANCE IS NEVER LABELLED LIVE-BOUND, anywhere. `_fixture.ts`'s mirror section
  348:    * binds SIGN-IN issuance â€” the live checks (a) and (b) measure a password grant and the
  349:    * `auth.sessions` row it creates â€” and says of registration issuance only that it diverges. A
  350:    * label claiming otherwise would be an untrue stated fact, and this paragraph is where the truth
  351:    * is kept.
  352:    *
  353:    * WHAT THE NEW BODIES DO ABOUT IT: they narrow it. AT-001.12, .13, .14 and .38 all follow the
  354:    * LIVE PUBLIC ORDER â€” register, use the emailed verification link, sign in, and only then play
  355:    * the session and password games â€” so the registration-minted handle plays no part in any
  356:    * assertion they make. The older bodies still use it, and AT-001.10's body relies on it
  357:    * deliberately: it builds a completed-but-unverified account, which the live public path cannot
  358:    * reach, because decision-8 makes verification the WRITE PATH's own floor rather than a property
  359:    * borrowed from the session layer. The live-stack behaviour is measured in
  360:    * `loop/items/AI4DEV-59/proof-local.ts` checks (a) and (c), and in this item's own proof.
  361:    */
  362:   registerWithEmailPassword(email: string, password: string): Promise<Session>;
  363:   /**
  364:    * A session Auth recorded as having come from an external provider.
  365:    *
  366:    * It performs NO handshake and fabricates no authorization code or token exchange: it is the state
  367:    * Auth is in AFTER a consent round trip, which is the only part of the story any agent can reach.
  368:    */
  369:   registerWithProvider(provider: SessionProvider, email: string): Promise<Session>;
  370:   /**
  371:    * A GitHub OAuth signup, as Auth records it: a session established by GitHub whose account already
  372:    * carries the linked GitHub identity â€” AT-001.02's "signs up via GitHub OAuth".
  373:    *
  374:    * Like `registerWithProvider` it performs NO handshake. It is `registerWithProvider('github', â€¦)`
  375:    * plus the fact that a GitHub signup necessarily links the GitHub identity it signed up with, and
  376:    * it is a separate member rather than an argument because that second half is what AT-001.02's
  377:    * "the GitHub identity is linked to it" clause is about.
  378:    */
  379:   registerWithGithub(email: string, githubHandle: string): Promise<Session>;
  380:   /**
  381:    * The state Auth is in after a `linkIdentity` round trip â€” AT-001.04's "linking completes signup".
  382:    *
  383:    * A LINKED IDENTITY IS NOT THE ESTABLISHING PROVIDER, and keeping the two apart is the point of
  384:    * this member existing at all: an email- or Google-established volunteer links GitHub and their
  385:    * session's `provider` does not change. The gate in
  386:    * `supabase/functions/_shared/accounts.ts` reads the link; nothing shipped reads the provider.
  387:    *
  388:    * Same posture as every other Auth member here â€” no handshake, no fabricated authorization code.
  389:    */
  390:   linkGithubIdentity(session: Session, githubHandle: string): Promise<void>;
  391:   /** Return sign-in with the same credentials â€” AT-001.01's final clause. */
  392:   signInWithEmailPassword(email: string, password: string): Promise<SignInOutcome>;
  393:   /**
  394:    * The return visit through an external provider â€” AT-001.02's "a later sign-in via GitHub returns
  395:    * to the same account".
  396:    *
  397:    * What it asserts is IDENTITY CONTINUITY, which is the half of that clause reachable without a
  398:    * person: an existing user whose account carries that provider's identity signs in to the SAME
  399:    * account rather than to a second one. The consent round trip itself is not performed and is named
  400:    * unproved in the plan's per-id table.
  401:    */
  402:   signInWithProvider(provider: SessionProvider, email: string): Promise<SignInOutcome>;
  403: 
  404:   /* ----------------------------- Supabase Auth's session layer -------------------------------- */
  405: 
  406:   /**
  407:    * End this session â€” the mirror of `POST /auth/v1/logout`, which deletes the `auth.sessions` row.
  408:    *
  409:    * IT ENDS ONE SESSION, NOT THE ACCOUNT'S ACCESS. That is the distinction AT-001.12's revocation
  410:    * half turns on: after it, work under this handle is refused and the account is otherwise
  411:    * untouched â€” a fresh sign-in works immediately, which is what makes re-authentication the
  412:    * REMEDY rather than a coincidence.
  413:    *
  414:    * SO IT MIRRORS THE `?scope=local` SHAPE, AND THE VENDOR'S DEFAULT IS THE OTHER ONE. Measured on
  415:    * the live stack and recorded in `loop/items/AI4DEV-60/proof-local.txt`: a plain
  416:    * `POST /auth/v1/logout` carries the `global` scope and ends EVERY session the user holds. The
  417:    * live counterpart of THIS member is therefore the scoped call, which is what check (c) makes â€”
  418:    * with a sibling session as the control, so "this session ended" and "the account's access ended"
  419:    * cannot be confused. No body in this suite asserts the difference: every one of them signs in
  420:    * afresh after a sign-out, which both scopes permit.
  421:    *
  422:    * It returns nothing, and a handle Auth never issued THROWS rather than answering politely: a
  423:    * body signing out a session that does not exist has a bug in the TEST, and a polite outcome
  424:    * would let that bug read as a product refusal further down. Same posture as
  425:    * `linkGithubIdentity`.
  426:    */
  427:   signOut(session: Session): Promise<void>;
  428:   /**
  429:    * Refresh a session â€” AT-001.13's act, and the whole point of it is what it does NOT take.
  430:    *
  431:    * NO CREDENTIALS PASS THROUGH THIS CALL, and that is structural rather than asserted: there is no
  432:    * parameter for a password. On the live stack the refresh token does this, which is why a client
  433:    * can keep a session alive while the user works and never ask them to sign in again.
  434:    *
  435:    * IT WORKS AFTER THE ACCESS TOKEN HAS EXPIRED, which is the mirror that matters â€” a refresh token
  436:    * outliving the access token is the entire mechanism. It stops working once the session is
  437:    * revoked, because revocation removes the session itself.
  438:    *
  439:    * IT EXTENDS THE SAME SESSION, and the returned handle carries the same `sessionId`. Whether the
  440:    * live vendor extends the row or rotates it is MEASURED rather than assumed â€”
