   60:  * ============================================================================================
   61:  * THE VENDOR MIRRORS, EACH WITH WHAT BINDS IT â€” named one by one, and never in a blanket claim.
   62:  * ============================================================================================
   63:  *
   64:  * A MIRROR IS NOT A PRODUCT JUDGEMENT, and keeping the two apart is what this section is for. The
   65:  * PRODUCT judgements on these paths â€” whether an address is verified, whether an unverified caller
   66:  * may send a Discovery message, and WHO IS CALLING â€” come from the shipped
   67:  * `supabase/functions/_shared/verification.ts` and `supabase/functions/_shared/caller.ts` and from
   68:  * nowhere else; there is no second copy of any of those rules in this file. What is left is a
   69:  * prediction of what Supabase Auth DOES, and a prediction has to say what would prove it wrong.
   70:  *
   71:  * EVERY ENTRY BELOW CARRIES EITHER A LIVE CHECK OR AN EXPLICIT UNBOUND LABEL. A blanket "all of
   72:  * these are live-bound" was the shape a gate-1 reviewer rejected, and correctly: it would let a
   73:  * mirror nothing measures sit under a label claiming something does. Where a check binds only part
   74:  * of a mirror, the label says which part.
   75:  *
   76:  * The first four are the verification leaf's. The session and reset mirrors follow, and the live
   77:  * checks they name are this item's own â€” `loop/items/AI4DEV-60/proof-local.ts`.
   78:  *
   79:  *   1. AN EMAIL/PASSWORD REGISTRATION STARTS UNCONFIRMED AND AN EMAIL CARRYING A VERIFICATION
   80:  *      LINK IS SENT.
   81:  *      BOUND â€” checks (a) and (b) of `loop/items/AI4DEV-59/proof-local.ts` on the live stack: the
   82:  *      signup's own wire response, `auth.users.email_confirmed_at IS NULL` read on the database,
   83:  *      and the confirmation email held by the local mail catcher.
   84:  *   2. A LINK THAT WAS NEVER ISSUED CONFIRMS NOTHING.
   85:  *      BOUND â€” check (b2) of `loop/items/AI4DEV-59/proof-local.ts`, and by that check alone. It
   86:  *      follows a TAMPERED variant of the real link â€” the token mutated, so a token GoTrue never
   87:  *      issued â€” BEFORE the real link is followed, and reads `auth.users.email_confirmed_at` still
   88:  *      NULL afterwards. This entry used to cite checks (a)-(d), which measure only the POSITIVE
   89:  *      half; two blind reviewers found that overstatement independently, so the check that
   90:  *      measures the negative was added rather than the label softened. Note what this is NOT: it
   91:  *      is not a claim about expiry, single use or resend. The token followed was never minted, so
   92:  *      no lifetime and no use count is in play. AT-001.11 is retired and none of those semantics
   93:  *      is modelled here.
   94:  *   3. USING THE EMAILED LINK SETS `email_confirmed_at`.
   95:  *      BOUND â€” checks (b) and (d): the link is followed by HTTP GET and the column flips to
   96:  *      non-null, after which the real `/auth/v1/user` response is fed to the SHIPPED
   97:  *      `emailVerifiedFromUser`, which answers true.
   98:  *   4. A PROVIDER REGISTRATION (Google, GitHub) STARTS CONFIRMED, because the provider vouched for
   99:  *      the address.
  100:  *      **UNBOUND, AND SAID PLAINLY.** No OAuth app or credential exists in this environment, so no
  101:  *      live provider session is obtainable â€” the same recorded gap that leaves the OAuth handshake
  102:  *      itself unproved at every tier, stated in this header since the predecessor item. This item
  103:  *      binds it nowhere and claims nothing about it. NO TEST IN THIS SUITE READS A PROVIDER USER'S
  104:  *      VERIFIED STATE: both verification bodies register by email and password. If the prediction
  105:  *      is wrong â€” a real provider user arriving unconfirmed â€” the shipped gate FAILS CLOSED: it
  106:  *      refuses that caller, never allows one. The mirror is bound by the first item that ships a
  107:  *      real provider-path consumer, and not before.
  108:  *
  109:  *   5. SIGNING IN MINTS A SESSION, WHICH IS A ROW IN `auth.sessions` WITH AN ACCESS TOKEN THAT
  110:  *      EXPIRES ONE HOUR LATER.
  111:  *      BOUND FOR SIGN-IN, AND FOR SIGN-IN ONLY â€” checks (a) and (b) of
  112:  *      `loop/items/AI4DEV-60/proof-local.ts`: a password grant with the correct password answers
  113:  *      200 with tokens and the `auth.sessions` row exists; a wrong-password attempt adds NO row â€”
  114:  *      measured as an unchanged session-id set across the refused attempt, one row already
  115:  *      standing from the confirmation-link implicit-flow sign-in (the re-pin in
  116:  *      `loop/items/AI4DEV-60/fix-rulings.md` ruling 1).
  117:  *      The one hour is `jwt_expiry = 3600` at `supabase/config.toml` line 165, and the constant
  118:  *      below cites that line.
  119:  *      WHAT THIS ENTRY DOES NOT COVER, said plainly because the earlier draft of it overreached:
  120:  *        - REGISTRATION issuance. This fixture mints a session at registration and the live stack
  121:  *          with confirmations on mints none. That is the DECLARED DIVERGENCE, written out in
  122:  *          `_contract.ts` on `registerWithEmailPassword`, and it is never labelled bound.
  123:  *        - PROVIDER issuance (Google, GitHub). **UNBOUND** â€” no OAuth app or credential exists in
  124:  *          this environment, the same recorded gap that leaves mirror 4 unbound. Nothing in this
  125:  *          suite asserts a provider session's expiry, revocation or refresh.
  126:  *        - ADMINISTRATOR issuance. **UNBOUND** â€” the recipe this repository records creates the
  127:  *          user through `POST /auth/v1/admin/users`, which issues NO session at all; the handle
  128:  *          `provisionPlatformAdmin` returns is this fixture's convenience so AT-001.07 has
  129:  *          something to act with. Nothing asserts an administrator's session layer.
  130:  *   6. LOGGING OUT ENDS THAT SESSION'S ACCESS BEFORE THE ACCESS TOKEN'S OWN EXPIRY.
  131:  *      BOUND â€” check (c): `POST /auth/v1/logout?scope=local` with a live token, then the SAME
  132:  *      access token at `/auth/v1/user` and at a DEPLOYED edge function, with the refusal code
  133:  *      captured verbatim. This is the load-bearing vendor claim of AT-001.12's revocation half â€” a
  134:  *      stateless reading of a JWT would say the token still works â€” so it is MEASURED and pinned
  135:  *      rather than assumed.
  136:  *      THE SCOPE IS NAMED BECAUSE THE VENDOR'S DEFAULT IS THE OTHER ONE. A plain logout, with no
  137:  *      scope, ends EVERY session the user holds (`global`); this mirror models one session ending
  138:  *      (`local`), and check (c) binds it with a sibling session standing as the control. Both
  139:  *      answers are in `proof-local.txt`, and no acceptance body asserts the difference.
  140:  *   7. AN ACCESS TOKEN STOPS WORKING AT ITS EXPIRY, AND A REFRESH RE-ESTABLISHES ACCESS WITHOUT
  141:  *      CREDENTIALS, INCLUDING AFTER THAT EXPIRY.
  142:  *      BOUND â€” check (d), against a transiently lowered `jwt_expiry`: the expired token is refused
  143:  *      at `/auth/v1/user` and at the deployed function, then the same session's refresh token
  144:  *      answers 200 with a fresh access token that works at both. The transient config change is
  145:  *      restored inside the same check and `git diff` proves `supabase/config.toml` unchanged.
  146:  *   8. A REFRESH EXTENDS THE SAME SESSION ROW RATHER THAN OPENING A NEW ONE.
  147:  *      BOUND BY A PROBE â€” check (d) reads `auth.sessions` for the user with operator authority
  148:  *      before and after the refresh and asserts the same row id, no new row. This entry exists
  149:  *      because check (d)'s main body proves refreshed ACCESS and says nothing about the row; a
  150:  *      gate-1 reviewer caught the gap. If the vendor rotates the row instead, the fail-and-re-pin
  151:  *      protocol applies and `refreshSession` below is corrected to the measurement.
  152:  *      NOT MODELLED AND NOT ASSERTED: refresh-token rotation and `refresh_token_reuse_interval`.
  153:  *      No criterion reads either.
  154:  *   9. ASKING FOR A PASSWORD RESET EMAILS A LINK, AND COMPLETING THAT LINK'S FLOW CHANGES THE
  155:  *      PASSWORD.
  156:  *      BOUND â€” check (e): a registered address gets a recovery email in the local catcher, the
  157:  *      link's flow shape is MEASURED before anything is asserted (token-in-fragment and a PKCE code
  158:  *      differ across CLI versions), the new password is set through the measured flow, the old
  159:  *      password is then refused with the code check (a) pinned, and the new one answers 200.
  160:  *  10. ASKING FOR A RESET ON AN ADDRESS NOBODY REGISTERED ANSWERS THE SAME WAY.
  161:  *      BOUND BY A PROBE â€” check (e) calls `/auth/v1/recover` once for a never-registered address
  162:  *      and captures the answer, expecting the same 200 no-existence-oracle shape. One HTTP call,
  163:  *      added because the rest of check (e) exercises an EXISTING address only.
  164:  *  11. A RESET LINK THAT WAS NEVER ISSUED CHANGES NOTHING.
  165:  *      BOUND â€” check (e)'s tampered probe, and by that alone: a mutated variant of the real link
  166:  *      is followed FIRST and the old password still signs in. Note what this is NOT â€” the token
  167:  *      followed was never minted, so no lifetime and no use count is in play. AT-001.15 is retired
  168:  *      and no expiry, single-use or resend semantics are modelled here.
  169:  *
  170:  * NOT MIRRORED AT ALL, and therefore asserted nowhere: `[auth.sessions]`'s timebox and inactivity
  171:  * timeout. Both sit COMMENTED OUT in `supabase/config.toml` (lines 304-308), no criterion reads
  172:  * them, and a mirror of a setting that is off would be a prediction about a configuration this
  173:  * repository does not run.
  174:  *
  175:  * THE PROVISIONED PLATFORM ADMINISTRATOR STARTS CONFIRMED, and it is a mirror of a RECIPE rather
  176:  * than of a live measurement. `provisionPlatformAdmin` below marks its auth user confirmed and
  177:  * mints no verification link, because creating an administrator sends no email. The recipe it
  178:  * mirrors is the only one this repository records: the predecessor item's live-proof script creates
  179:  * that user through `POST /auth/v1/admin/users` with `email_confirm: true`. The earlier version of
  180:  * this fixture let the administrator start UNCONFIRMED by reusing the public email path, which
  181:  * contradicted that record.
  182:  * WHAT BINDS IT: nothing yet, and that is said plainly. NOTHING READS AN ADMINISTRATOR'S VERIFIED
  183:  * STATE â€” not AT-001.07, not anything else in this suite. The predecessor's transcript ran with
  184:  * confirmations OFF, so it does not measure the column under this leaf's flip either. So this
  185:  * mirror is labelled by its recipe and is never called live-bound; the first item that reads an
  186:  * administrator's verified state is the one that binds it.
  187:  *
  188:  * AND THE DISCOVERY SEND SURFACE IS NOT A MIRROR AT ALL â€” it mirrors nothing, because there is
  189:  * nothing to mirror. No Discovery route exists in this repository at either tier; it is
  190:  * REQ-002/004's. `sendDiscoveryMessage` below is a stand-in surface whose whole job is to put the
  191:  * shipped gate on a tested path. No green over it says anything about enforcement anywhere.
  192:  */
  193: 
  194: import type { ControlledClock } from '../../harness/clock.ts';
