  436:     /**
  437:      * THE CACHED TOKENS SURVIVE THE LOGOUT, DELIBERATELY (gate-2 ruling S2-2).
  438:      *
  439:      * This method used to end with `sessions.delete(session.sessionId)`, and that one line made the
  440:      * revocation clause untestable: the next call with the same handle threw client-side in
  441:      * `tokensOf` before any request left this process, so the assertion "a revoked session cannot
  442:      * write" was measuring THIS FILE'S bookkeeping rather than the live stack's judgement. A test
  443:      * that passes because the client refused to try has proved nothing about the server.
  444:      *
  445:      * So the handle keeps its tokens and the post-logout write really goes out, carrying a token
  446:      * that is revoked and not yet expired. Whatever the live stack answers is the measured fact.
  447:      *
  448:      * THE DIVERGENCE HANDLE IS UNTOUCHED. `tokensOf` still refuses a handle that never held a
  449:      * session â€” a registration under confirmations â€” which is a different case from a session that
  450:      * existed and was revoked, and is the one the file header describes.
  451:      */
