  246:  * Exit 0 â€” through the bound `atTest`, the raw `atTest` and `defineEvidenceCapture` alike, with no
  247:  * cast, no `any`, no suppression and no module augmentation. NOTHING IN THE TYPE SYSTEM CAN STOP IT,
  248:  * for the reason two paragraphs up: the widened type and the derived one are assignable in both
  249:  * directions, so no annotation the harness writes can tell them apart. Closing it means INSPECTING
  250:  * SOURCE â€” permitting only inline, unannotated bodies â€” which is a different kind of machinery,
  251:  * filed as its own item rather than built here (AI4DEV-37).
  252:  *
  253:  * The line is worth stating as a rule rather than as an excuse. What is closed is a suite NAMING the
  254:  * seam types, and with it every route the API used to invite. What is open is a hand-written
  255:  * structural reconstruction, which is a decision somebody takes rather than a mistake they make.
  256:  * `tests/at/typeprobes/sut-seam.probe.ts` carries it verbatim as a documented known-open case (it
  257:  * cannot be an active probe there, because that program must not compile and this attack does), and
  258:  * `loop/items/AI4DEV-31/gate2-widen-reproduction.txt` is the compile transcript with its controls.
  259:  */
  260: type SeamOpenWorld<Sut = unknown, W extends WorldLike = WorldLike, T extends Tier = 'loop'> = {
  261:   /**
  262:    * The harness, at EXACTLY the type `createHarness()` is statically checked to produce â€” not a
  263:    * suite-chosen type, and not the suite's type arguments pushed back into it.
  264:    *
  265:    * Two doors are shut here, and both were opened by earlier drafts of this file. A free type
  266:    * parameter (`H extends HarnessLike`) let a suite declare seams the factory never supplies, since
  267:    * an intersection is a subtype and satisfies the constraint. Re-labelling the produced harness
  268:    * with the suite's own world and channel types was the same defect wearing a smaller hat: the
  269:    * factory proves `AtHarness<â€¦, WorldSeam, string>`, so a suite binding channel `'sms'` would have
  270:    * been told `vendors.email.attempts()` yields `'sms'` on the strength of nothing at all.
  271:    *
  272:    * `h` is deliberately NOT re-parameterized by AI4DEV-31 either. Deriving `w` and `sut` from the
  273:    * adapter does not license pushing them back into the harness: that is the same door, and it
  274:    * stays shut.
  275:    *
  276:    * IT IS PARAMETERIZED BY THE TIER, AND THAT IS NOT THE SAME DOOR. `T` is not a suite's type
  277:    * argument: a suite never writes it, and the only two values it takes come from which key of a
  278:    * per-tier body map a body was written under. `TierHarness<T>` then SUBTRACTS â€” at integration the
  279:    * clock loses its control seam and the vendors seam loses its arming methods â€” so the parameter
  280:    * can only ever narrow what a body may reach. A free harness type parameter widened; this one
  281:    * cannot, because the mapping from `T` to the harness type is written here and there is no `T` a
  282:    * suite can supply that adds a member.
  283:    */
  284:   h: TierHarness<T>;
  285:   /**
  286:    * The fixture world, at the type the requirement's ADAPTER really returns â€” `WorldOf<R>`, read
  287:    * off `fixtures.world`'s return type in `suite-adapters.ts`.
  288:    *
  289:    * It used to be the suite's own type argument, asserted rather than verified, so
  290:    * `bindSuite<NotificationsSut, AnythingAtAll>` type-checked green and a body could read members
  291:    * no fixture supplies. `tests/at/typeprobes/sut-seam-legacy.probe.ts` is that attack, kept alive:
  292:    * it compiled clean before this change and must fail now.
  293:    */
  294:   w: W;
  295:   /**
  296:    * The requirement's system under test, at the type its ADAPTER declares (`SutOf<R, K>`), and
  297:    * guaranteed non-null once open() returns.
  298:    *
  299:    * WHAT IS ACTUALLY CHECKED, precisely, because a closure claim wider than the truth is the defect
  300:    * this item removes: the adapter annotates what it builds (`const sut: NotificationsSut = â€¦`), so
  301:    * the shape is proven at the producer; the suite reads that type instead of restating it; the
  302:    * `requirement` literal ties the map entry, the module the type came from and the module
  303:    * `loadAdapter()` really imports to one self-declared value, checked at compile time AND again at
  304:    * run time. One assertion survives, at the end of `open()`, and it is commented there.
  305:    *
  306:    * WHAT IS NOT CHECKED: `any`, `as`, `@ts-ignore`, `@ts-nocheck`, mutating the adapter's object at
  307:    * run time, pointing `AT_REPO_ROOT` at a different tree, and REBUILDING A WIDENED CONTEXT BY HAND
  308:    * out of the derived types (measured, and documented on `SeamOpenWorld` above) all still work. The
  309:    * threat model is a suite DRIFTING from its harness with nobody able to notice â€” an honest mistake
  310:    * that type-checks green â€” not an author set on defeating the type system, who can always write a
  311:    * cast, and who now has to write something at least as deliberate as one.
  312:    */
  313:   sut: Sut;
  314: };
  315: 
  316: /**
  317:  * What `open()` hands a test body, for the suite bound to requirement `R` and sut key `K`.
  318:  *
  319:  * Named by TWO STRINGS, never by a shape â€” see `SeamOpenWorld` above for why that distinction is
  320:  * the whole protection rather than a stylistic preference.
  321:  */
  322: export type OpenWorld<R extends SuiteId, K extends SutKeyOf<R>, T extends Tier = 'loop'> = SeamOpenWorld<
  323:   SutOf<R, K>,
  324:   WorldOf<R>,
  325:   T
  326: >;
  327: 
  328: export interface OpenOverrides {
  329:   /**
  330:    * Re-tune pinned values for THIS world only, so one authored body can be run against two
  331:    * materially different configurations. A key the at-config registry does not already carry
  332:    * throws: an override may re-tune a knob that exists, never invent one the product has not
  333:    * got.
  334:    */
  335:   config?: ConfigOverrides;
  336: }
  337: 
  338: /** The STRUCTURE of what a test body is given â€” not exported, for the reason on `SeamOpenWorld`. */
  339: type SeamContext<Sut = unknown, W extends WorldLike = WorldLike, T extends Tier = 'loop'> = {
  340:   atId: string;
  341:   /** build a fresh "Given" world (and its own harness). Call it more than once for isolation. */
  342:   open(fixture?: string, opts?: OpenOverrides): Promise<SeamOpenWorld<Sut, W, T>>;
  343:   /** consume an immutable capture whose producer proved at least one real open() */
  344:   capture<C>(evidence: EvidenceCaptureImpl<C, Sut, W, T>): Promise<C>;
  345: };
  346: 
  347: /** Everything a test body is given. `atId` is read-only context, never re-supplied to open(). */
  348: export type AtContext<R extends SuiteId, K extends SutKeyOf<R>, T extends Tier = 'loop'> = SeamContext<
  349:   SutOf<R, K>,
  350:   WorldOf<R>,
  351:   T
  352: >;
  353: 
  354: const USAGE = Symbol('at-context-usage');
  355: 
  356: interface Usage {
  357:   opens: number;
  358:   captures: number;
  359: }
  360: 
  361: /**
  362:  * NOT EXPORTED, and a type alias rather than an interface, for the two different reasons this file
  363:  * keeps apart: not exported so no suite can name it, an alias so nothing can merge a member into it
  364:  * even from inside this module. It is reachable from a test body â€” `capture()` hands it to an
  365:  * evidence producer â€” and everything on that path obeys the alias rule.
  366:  */
  367: type InternalContext<Sut, W extends WorldLike, T extends Tier = 'loop'> = SeamContext<Sut, W, T> & {
  368:   [USAGE]: Usage;
  369: };
  370: 
  371: /** Where in the captured structure a value sits, said in words a test author can act on. */
  372: function evidencePath(path: string): string {
  373:   return path ? `the captured value at ${JSON.stringify(path)}` : 'the captured value';
  374: }
  375: 
  376: function constructorName(value: object): string {
  377:   return Object.getPrototypeOf(value)?.constructor?.name ?? 'an object with an exotic prototype';
  378: }
  379: 
  380: /**
  381:  * Deep-freeze a capture â€” and REFUSE anything that freezing does not actually make immutable.
  382:  *
  383:  * `Object.freeze` is shallow in a second sense nobody expects: it seals a Map's own properties
  384:  * and leaves `set`/`delete` fully working, and the same is true of a Set, a Date and any class
  385:  * instance with mutating methods. So a capture carrying one of those LOOKS frozen and is not â€”
  386:  * which defeats the whole point of capture-once/assert-many, where one lens normalizing or
  387:  * de-duplicating evidence in place would hide it from the next lens. Silently freezing such a
  388:  * value is worse than rejecting it, because the immutability the suites are told to rely on
  389:  * would be a lie in exactly the cases that matter.
  390:  *
  391:  * So the accepted shapes are the ones freezing genuinely closes: primitives, arrays, and plain
  392:  * objects (`Object.prototype` or a null prototype â€” what `structuredClone` and object literals
  393:  * produce). Everything else throws, naming the path, so the producer converts it to inert data
  394:  * (a Date to an ISO string, a Map to entries) where the conversion is visible.
  395:  */
  396: export function freezeEvidence<T>(value: T, path = '', seen = new WeakSet<object>()): T {
  397:   if (value === null) return value;
  398:   const type = typeof value;
  399:   if (type === 'undefined' || type === 'string' || type === 'number' || type === 'boolean' || type === 'bigint') return value;
  400:   if (type === 'function') {
  401:     throw new Error(`${evidencePath(path)} is a function â€” captured evidence must be inert data, not something that can still run`);
  402:   }
  403:   if (type === 'symbol') {
  404:     throw new Error(`${evidencePath(path)} is a symbol â€” captured evidence must be inert data an assertion can read`);
  405:   }
  406: 
  407:   const object = value as unknown as object;
  408:   if (seen.has(object)) return value;
  409:   seen.add(object);
  410: 
  411:   if (Array.isArray(object)) {
  412:     for (const [index, child] of object.entries()) freezeEvidence(child, `${path}[${index}]`, seen);
  413:     return Object.freeze(value);
  414:   }
  415: 
  416:   const proto = Object.getPrototypeOf(object);
  417:   if (proto !== null && proto !== Object.prototype) {
  418:     throw new Error(
  419:       `${evidencePath(path)} is a ${constructorName(object)} â€” freezeEvidence accepts only primitives, ` +
  420:         `arrays and plain objects, because Object.freeze leaves a Map, Set, Date or class instance ` +
  421:         `mutable through its own methods. Capture it as inert data instead.`,
  422:     );
  423:   }
  424: 
  425:   for (const [key, child] of Object.entries(object as Record<string, unknown>)) {
  426:     freezeEvidence(child, path ? `${path}.${key}` : key, seen);
  427:   }
  428:   return Object.freeze(value);
  429: }
  430: 
  431: export function captureProducerProblem(opensBefore: number, opensAfter: number): string | null {
  432:   return opensAfter === opensBefore ? 'capture producer completed without open()' : null;
  433: }
  434: 
  435: /**
  436:  * The implementation class, DELIBERATELY NOT EXPORTED â€” only the type alias below is.
  437:  *
  438:  * An exported class is a value with a public constructor, so `new EvidenceCapture<T, Anything,
  439:  * AnythingElse>(name, producer)` was a way to hand a producer a fabricated `AtContext` without ever
  440:  * touching `bindSuite`: the same lie as the one AI4DEV-31 closes at `open()`, reached through a
  441:  * `new`. Exporting only the type removes the constructor from a suite's reach, and has the second
  442:  * effect of making `EvidenceCapture` a type ALIAS, which declaration merging cannot open.
  443:  */
  444: /**
  445:  * WHAT A FAILING SHARED PRODUCER THROWS â€” and the two refusals it must NOT dress up.
  446:  *
  447:  * PURE AND EXPORTED, so the rule has an oracle: it is a rule about what a red LOOKS like, and a
  448:  * rule about a shape that nothing compares is a rule nobody knows works.
  449:  *
  450:  * The wrapper exists so an ordinary failure inside a shared producer says WHICH capture failed and
  451:  * whose, instead of surfacing on five consumer ids as an unattributed error. That is right for an
  452:  * ordinary failure and wrong for a REFUSAL. `CapabilityPending` and `AtPending` are the two red
  453:  * shapes a declaration can describe, and `expected.ts` rebuilds their first line and compares it
  454:  * exactly. Wrapping one turns `CapabilityPending: CAPABILITY PENDING â€” â€¦` into
  455:  * `Error: evidence capture "â€¦" failed â€” CAPABILITY PENDING â€” â€¦`, which no declaration can express.
  456:  * Measured on REQ-016's first integration run: five ids were red in a shape nobody could declare,
  457:  * which is the exact defect the declarable refusal exists to remove.
  458:  *
  459:  * Nothing is hidden by passing them through: both carry their own names, and the id that reports
  460:  * one is the id that leaned on it.
  461:  */
  462: export function captureFailure(name: string, requirement: string, producerAtId: string, err: unknown): unknown {
  463:   if (err instanceof CapabilityPending || err instanceof AtPending) return err;
  464:   const detail = err instanceof Error ? err.message : String(err);
  465:   return new Error(
  466:     `evidence capture ${JSON.stringify(name)} (${requirement}) produced by ${producerAtId} failed â€” ${detail}`,
  467:     { cause: err },
  468:   );
  469: }
  470: 
  471: class EvidenceCaptureImpl<C, Sut = unknown, W extends WorldLike = WorldLike, T extends Tier = 'loop'> {
  472:   private result: Promise<C> | null = null;
  473:   private producerAtId = '';
  474: 
  475:   constructor(
  476:     readonly name: string,
  477:     /** the suite this capture reads the seam of â€” it is in the failure text so a red says whose */
  478:     readonly requirement: string,
  479:     private readonly producer: (ctx: SeamContext<Sut, W, T>) => Promise<C>,
  480:   ) {}
  481: 
  482:   consume(ctx: InternalContext<Sut, W, T>): Promise<C> {
  483:     if (!this.result) {
  484:       this.producerAtId = ctx.atId;
  485:       const opensBefore = ctx[USAGE].opens;
  486:       this.result = (async () => {
  487:         try {
  488:           const value = await this.producer(ctx);
  489:           const problem = captureProducerProblem(opensBefore, ctx[USAGE].opens);
  490:           if (problem) {
  491:             throw new Error(`evidence capture ${JSON.stringify(this.name)} produced by ${ctx.atId} â€” ${problem}`);
  492:           }
  493:           return freezeEvidence(value);
  494:         } catch (err) {
  495:           throw captureFailure(this.name, this.requirement, this.producerAtId, err);
  496:         }
  497:       })();
  498:     }
  499:     return this.result;
  500:   }
  501: }
  502: 
  503: /**
  504:  * An immutable capture, consumable by any test in the suite that defined it.
  505:  *
  506:  * A TYPE, never the class â€” and named by a requirement and a sut key, never by a shape. Both halves
  507:  * matter: the class would expose a constructor that takes a producer at any types, and shape
  508:  * parameters would let a body widen the context it is handed. See `SeamOpenWorld`.
  509:  */
  510: export type EvidenceCapture<T, R extends SuiteId, K extends SutKeyOf<R>> = EvidenceCaptureImpl<T, SutOf<R, K>, WorldOf<R>>;
  511: 
  512: /**
  513:  * `defineEvidenceCapture` was the SECOND door to the seam AI4DEV-31 closes, and closing `bindSuite`
  514:  * alone would have moved the defect rather than removed it: this function handed its producer a
  515:  * full `AtContext<Sut, W>` at whatever types the caller named, so a suite that never called
  516:  * `bindSuite` could still declare a system under test and a world out of nothing and read both green.
  517:  *
  518:  * So it takes the BINDING and derives, exactly as `atTest` does. There is no type argument left to
  519:  * lie with: `SutOf<R, K>` and `WorldOf<R>` come from the adapter registered under `R`.
  520:  *
  521:  * A `const` typed by a call-signature alias, NOT an exported `function` declaration â€” that is not
  522:  * stylistic. Gate 1 measured that an exported function declaration can be given a fresh generic
  523:  * overload through `declare module`, restoring the fabricated type argument, and that type aliases
  524:  * do not protect it. A `const` has no declaration to merge an overload into.
  525:  */
  526: type DefineEvidenceCaptureFn = <T, R extends SuiteId, K extends SutKeyOf<R>>(
  527:   binding: SuiteBinding<R, K>,
  528:   name: string,
  529:   producer: (ctx: AtContext<R, K>) => Promise<T>,
  530: ) => EvidenceCapture<T, R, K>;
  531: 
  532: export const defineEvidenceCapture: DefineEvidenceCaptureFn = (binding, name, producer) =>
  533:   new EvidenceCaptureImpl(name, binding.requirement, producer);
  534: 
  535: export function testUseProblem(opens: number, captures: number): string | null {
  536:   return opens === 0 && captures === 0 ? 'test body never opened a fixture world or consumed trusted captured evidence' : null;
  537: }
  538: 
  539: /**
  540:  * Runs one body and refuses a pass that observed nothing.
  541:  *
  542:  * Still parameterized by SHAPE rather than by `<R, K>`, and that is not an oversight. It is not a
  543:  * door to the seam: it never produces a harness-backed value, so the only context it can be given
  544:  * is one the caller built by hand â€” fabricating a type there is a caller lying to itself about its
  545:  * own object, with no harness claim anywhere in it. Keeping the structural signature is also what
  546:  * lets `conformance.selftest.ts` drive it with a hand-built context, which is the point of the
  547:  * false-green reproduction it appears in.
  548:  */
  549: export async function executeRegisteredBody<Sut, W extends WorldLike>(
  550:   atId: string,
  551:   body: (ctx: SeamContext<Sut, W>) => Promise<void>,
  552:   ctx: SeamContext<Sut, W>,
  553:   usage: Usage,
  554: ): Promise<void> {
  555:   await body(ctx);
  556:   const problem = testUseProblem(usage.opens, usage.captures);
  557:   if (problem) throw new Error(`${atId} INVALID â€” ${problem}`);
  558: }
  559: 
  560: /* ------------------------------------------------------------------------ teardown accounting */
  561: 
  562: /** One thing a test built and must give back, named so a failure says which one. */
  563: export interface TrackedTeardown {
  564:   /** plain words for the thing being torn down, e.g. `fixture world "req-016/base"` */
  565:   what: string;
  566:   teardown(): Promise<void>;
  567: }
  568: 
  569: export interface TeardownFailure {
  570:   what: string;
  571:   error: unknown;
  572: }
  573: 
  574: function errorText(error: unknown): string {
  575:   return error instanceof Error ? error.message : String(error);
  576: }
  577: 
  578: /**
  579:  * Tear down everything the test built â€” worlds first, then harnesses, last-opened-first within
  580:  * each â€” ATTEMPTING EVERY ONE and collecting the rejections rather than stopping at the first.
  581:  *
  582:  * Attempting all of them is the point: giving up after one rejection leaves the rest of the stack
  583:  * standing, and a frozen clock, an armed fault or a vendor counter that survives into the next id
  584:  * is exactly the cross-test leak the per-test teardown exists to prevent.
  585:  */
  586: export async function drainTeardowns(worlds: TrackedTeardown[], harnesses: TrackedTeardown[]): Promise<TeardownFailure[]> {
  587:   const failures: TeardownFailure[] = [];
  588:   const drain = async (stack: TrackedTeardown[]) => {
  589:     while (stack.length) {
  590:       const entry = stack.pop()!;
  591:       try {
  592:         await entry.teardown();
  593:       } catch (err) {
  594:         failures.push({ what: entry.what, error: err });
  595:       }
  596:     }
  597:   };
  598:   await drain(worlds);
  599:   await drain(harnesses);
  600:   return failures;
  601: }
  602: 
  603: /**
  604:  * One acceptance test's whole lifecycle: run the body, tear everything down, then decide.
  605:  *
  606:  * WHY THE VERDICT LIVES HERE rather than in a `finally` that swallows teardown rejections â€”
  607:  * which is what this used to do: a world whose teardown rejected did NOT release what it holds,
  608:  * so the next id inherits it. Reporting that as green is a false green of the worst kind, because
  609:  * the damage lands on a different test and the report blames the wrong id.
  610:  *
  611:  * When the body already failed, the body's error still wins the report: it is the more specific
  612:  * fact, and a teardown that rejects because the body left the world half-built is a symptom, not
  613:  * the cause. The teardown failures are printed so they are never invisible.
  614:  */
  615: export async function runTrackedTest(
  616:   atId: string,
  617:   run: () => Promise<void>,
  618:   worlds: TrackedTeardown[],
  619:   harnesses: TrackedTeardown[],
  620: ): Promise<void> {
  621:   let bodyFailed = false;
  622:   let bodyError: unknown;
  623:   try {
  624:     await run();
  625:   } catch (err) {
  626:     bodyFailed = true;
  627:     bodyError = err;
  628:   }
  629: 
  630:   const failures = await drainTeardowns(worlds, harnesses);
  631: 
  632:   if (bodyFailed) {
  633:     for (const failure of failures) {
  634:       console.error(`${atId} â€” teardown ALSO failed (${failure.what}): ${errorText(failure.error)}`);
  635:     }
  636:     throw bodyError;
  637:   }
  638: 
  639:   if (failures.length) {
  640:     const summary = failures.map((failure) => `${failure.what}: ${errorText(failure.error)}`).join('; ');
  641:     throw new AggregateError(
  642:       failures.map((failure) => (failure.error instanceof Error ? failure.error : new Error(errorText(failure.error)))),
  643:       `${atId} INVALID â€” the body passed but ${failures.length} teardown${failures.length === 1 ? '' : 's'} failed, ` +
  644:         `so state this test built leaks into the next id: ${summary}`,
  645:     );
  646:   }
  647: }
  648: 
  649: interface OpenOptions {
  650:   atId: string;
  651:   requirement: string;
  652:   sutKey: string;
  653:   sutMissing: string;
  654:   fixture: string;
  655:   configOverrides?: ConfigOverrides;
  656: }
  657: 
  658: /**
  659:  * Builds a harness and a world, at the types the HARNESS can actually prove: `sut` is `unknown`
  660:  * (`h.sut[key]` on a concrete `AtHarness`) and `w` is a bare `WorldLike`. It deliberately knows
  661:  * nothing about any suite â€” the narrowing to that suite's derived types happens once, at the end of
  662:  * `open()`, where the comment says exactly what is still taken on trust.
  663:  */
  664: async function openWorld(o: OpenOptions): Promise<{ opened: SeamOpenWorld; harness: AtHarness }> {
  665:   if (TIER === null) throw new AtPending(o.atId, 'tier-unset', tierError(o.requirement));
  666: 
  667:   if (!harnessModule) {
  668:     throw new AtPending(
  669:       o.atId,
  670:       'harness-missing',
  671:       `AI4DEV-3 capability modules (H2 fixtures/clock, H3 sentinels/faults, H4 oracles, ` +
  672:         `H5 vendor sims) are not in the tree: cannot resolve "${HARNESS_MODULE}" ` +
  673:         `from tests/at/harness (${harnessResolveError})`,
  674:     );
  675:   }
  676: 
  677:   const h = await harnessModule.createHarness({
  678:     requirement: `req-${o.requirement}`,
  679:     tier: TIER,
  680:     configOverrides: o.configOverrides,
  681:   });
  682:   // Tracked from here on: every later failure must still tear the harness down.
  683:   try {
  684:     expect(h.tier, `harness built tier "${h.tier}" for a --tier ${TIER} run`).toBe(TIER);
  685: 
  686:     /*
  687:      * TIER SEMANTICS: above `loop`, nothing the suite leans on may be a stand-in. The RULE is
  688:      * unchanged and is not relaxed by one capability. What changed is the SHAPE OF THE REFUSAL.
  689:      *
  690:      * This was a bare `expect(await h.stubbedCapabilities()).toEqual([])`, and the failure it
  691:      * produced fit NEITHER declarable red kind â€” so an integration run of any suite was not merely
  692:      * red, it was UNDECLARABLE, and `--expect` could never be honoured at the tier that is the
  693:      * closing gate. A red nobody can describe exactly is a red nobody understands, which is the
  694:      * doctrine `expected.ts` states and this line contradicted.
  695:      *
  696:      * `CapabilityPending` names the exact stubbed capabilities, which is precisely the
  697:      * `capability-pending` shape a declaration rebuilds and compares from position 0. The gate is if
  698:      * anything STRICTER than before: the names travel into the report, so a declaration has to say
  699:      * WHICH capabilities are still stubbed rather than only that some are.
  700:      *
  701:      * `expect.hasAssertions()` is satisfied by the throw rather than by an assertion, exactly as it
  702:      * is for every `AtPending` body in every suite: a thrown error is the reported failure and the
  703:      * assertion count is not consulted.
  704:      */
  705:     const stubRefusal = aboveLoopStubbedRefusal(TIER, await h.stubbedCapabilities());
  706:     if (stubRefusal) throw stubRefusal;
  707: 
  708:     const sut = h.sut?.[o.sutKey];
  709:     if (!sut) throw new AtPending(o.atId, 'sut-missing', o.sutMissing);
  710: 
  711:     const w = await h.fixtures.world(o.fixture);
  712:     return { opened: { h, w, sut }, harness: h };
  713:   } catch (err) {
  714:     await h.teardown().catch(() => undefined);
  715:     throw err;
  716:   }
  717: }
  718: 
  719: /* ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- registration */
  720: 
  721: /** One authored test body, for the suite bound to requirement `R` and sut key `K`, at tier `T`. */
  722: export type AtTestBody<R extends SuiteId, K extends SutKeyOf<R>, T extends Tier = 'loop'> = (
  723:   ctx: AtContext<R, K, T>,
  724: ) => Promise<void>;
  725: 
  726: /**
  727:  * ONE BODY PER ID PER TIER â€” the per-tier body form.
  728:  *
  729:  * WHY IT EXISTS. Some acceptance criteria are proved by DIFFERENT PROCEDURES at different tiers,
  730:  * proving the same criterion. AT-001.12's expiry arm at loop tier commands a controlled clock
  731:  * forward; at integration there is nothing to command, and the same criterion is proved by waiting
  732:  * out a real access token against a real GoTrue. Writing one body that branched on the tier would put
  733:  * two procedures inside one function with a conditional deciding which claim was being made â€” and a
  734:  * body that reads `if (h.tier === â€¦)` is a body whose green means something different depending on a
  735:  * value the reader has to trace.
  736:  *
  737:  * WHAT DOES NOT FORK. The CRITERION never forks: both bodies carry the same acceptance text in their
  738:  * title, and the id is registered once. What forks is the procedure and, with it, the capabilities
  739:  * the procedure may reach â€” which is what the per-tier context types carry.
  740:  *
  741:  * EVERY TIER MUST BE COVERED, and that is refused rather than defaulted. `analyzeReportedTests()`
  742:  * requires EXACTLY ONE runtime registration and EXACTLY ONE vitest result per expected id; a map
  743:  * that named only `loop` would emit neither at integration, and the id would be reported `missing` â€”
  744:  * a state no declaration can describe. So a map either names every tier or supplies `default` for
  745:  * the ones it does not, and a map that leaves a hole is an error AT THE CALL SITE, where it is
  746:  * written, rather than a missing row three commands later.
  747:  */
  748: export type AtTestBodies<R extends SuiteId, K extends SutKeyOf<R>> = {
  749:   /**
  750:    * The body for every tier this map does not name â€” typed at the LOOP tier, exactly as the
  751:    * single-body form is, because it is the single-body form wearing a key. See `AtTestFn` for why
  752:    * that is the tier the shared shape is written at, and what it does and does not protect.
  753:    */
  754:   default?: AtTestBody<R, K, 'loop'>;
  755:   loop?: AtTestBody<R, K, 'loop'>;
  756:   integration?: AtTestBody<R, K, 'integration'>;
  757:   drill?: AtTestBody<R, K, 'drill'>;
  758: };
  759: 
  760: /**
  761:  * The tier's body, or the problem that means there is none. PURE, so the refusal is unit testable.
  762:  *
  763:  * A bare function is the single-body form and covers every tier â€” every suite in this tree is
  764:  * written that way and none of them changes.
  765:  */
  766: export function tierBodyProblem(bodies: Record<string, unknown>, atId: string): string | null {
  767:   const named = TIERS.filter((tier) => typeof bodies[tier] === 'function');
  768:   if (named.length === 0 && typeof bodies.default !== 'function') {
  769:     return (
  770:       `${atId} was registered with a per-tier body map that names no body at all â€” neither a tier nor a default. ` +
  771:       `One id, one body per tier.`
  772:     );
  773:   }
  774:   const uncovered = TIERS.filter((tier) => typeof bodies[tier] !== 'function');
  775:   if (uncovered.length && typeof bodies.default !== 'function') {
  776:     return (
  777:       `${atId} was registered with a per-tier body map that covers ${named.join(', ')} but not ` +
  778:       `${uncovered.join(', ')}, and supplies no default. An id with no body at a tier reports as MISSING there, ` +
  779:       `which no declaration can describe â€” supply a body for every tier, or a default.`
  780:     );
  781:   }
  782:   return null;
  783: }
  784: 
  785: /**
  786:  * WHICH body a per-tier map supplies for a tier. PURE, so the choice is unit testable without
  787:  * registering anything with vitest.
  788:  *
  789:  * `null` for the tier means the map named a body for a DIFFERENT tier and no default â€” which
  790:  * `tierBodyProblem` refuses before this is ever consulted in the real path. It is returned rather
  791:  * than thrown so the selftest can state the rule positively: a body written for one tier does not
  792:  * run at another.
  793:  */
  794: export function chooseTierBody<B>(bodies: Record<string, B | undefined>, tier: Tier | null): B | null {
  795:   const named = tier === null ? undefined : bodies[tier];
  796:   return named ?? bodies.default ?? bodies.loop ?? null;
  797: }
  798: 
  799: /**
  800:  * THE ABOVE-LOOP STAND-IN REFUSAL, as a value rather than as a statement inside `openWorld`.
  801:  *
  802:  * It is exported and pure for one reason: `expected.ts` rebuilds the text a declared
  803:  * `capability-pending` red must produce, and the ONLY way to know the two agree is to compare them.
  804:  * `live-ledger.selftest.ts` does exactly that, so a wording change on either side breaks a test
  805:  * instead of silently making every integration declaration unmatchable.
  806:  */
  807: export function aboveLoopStubbedRefusal(tier: Tier, stubbed: readonly string[]): CapabilityPending | null {
  808:   if (tier === 'loop' || stubbed.length === 0) return null;
  809:   return new CapabilityPending([...stubbed]);
  810: }
  811: 
  812: function emitRuntimeRegistration(registration: Registration): void {
  813:   const dir = process.env.AT_REGISTRATION_DIR;
  814:   if (!dir) return;
  815:   mkdirSync(dir, { recursive: true });
  816:   appendFileSync(join(dir, `registration-${process.pid}.jsonl`), `${JSON.stringify(registration)}\n`, 'utf8');
  817: }
  818: 
  819: /**
  820:  * THE ONE PLACE the AT id's requirement and the binding's requirement are made to agree.
  821:  *
  822:  * They are two independent strings that must denote the same suite: the id decides which fixture
  823:  * adapter `createHarness()` loads at RUN time (`openWorld` passes `parsed.requirement` straight
  824:  * through), while the binding decides which adapter the TYPES were read off at compile time. Left
  825:  * unchecked, `AT-017.03` registered through a suite bound to `req-016` would drive req-017's
  826:  * implementation while every type in the body described req-016's, and both halves would look fine.
  827:  *
  828:  * The formats differ and the comparison must normalize, or it would reject every valid suite:
  829:  * `parseAtId('AT-016.01').requirement` is `"016"`, whereas the binding and the registry key are
  830:  * `"req-016"`.
  831:  *
  832:  * The field stays MANDATORY. Making the check fire only when a requirement happens to be supplied
  833:  * would reopen the hole through the door marked convenience â€” so a binding with no requirement is
  834:  * its own error, not a skipped check.
  835:  */
  836: export function requirementMismatch(atId: string, parsedRequirement: string, bound: string): string | null {
  837:   if (!bound) {
  838:     return (
  839:       `${atId} was registered with no requirement â€” atTest cannot check that the suite whose types ` +
  840:       `were used is the suite whose fixture adapter will be loaded. Bind the suite with ` +
  841:       `bindSuite({ requirement: 'req-${parsedRequirement}', sut: â€¦ }).`
  842:     );
  843:   }
  844:   const fromId = `req-${parsedRequirement}`;
  845:   if (fromId === bound) return null;
  846:   return (
  847:     `${atId} was registered through a suite bound to ${bound} â€” at run time the harness would load ` +
  848:     `${fromId}'s fixture adapter, because the requirement comes from the AT id, while the ` +
  849:     `type-check described ${bound}'s. One id, one suite: correct the id or correct the binding.`
  850:   );
  851: }
  852: 
  853: /**
  854:  * Register one acceptance test. The id is declared here and nowhere else.
  855:  *
  856:  * - a malformed id throws AT REGISTRATION â€” the id is checked where it is written.
  857:  * - the id's requirement must match the suite's binding, for the reason above.
  858:  * - `expect.hasAssertions()` makes a body that asserts nothing a RED, so a tagged test cannot
  859:  *   satisfy the bijection checker by existing.
  860:  * - worlds AND harnesses are tracked per test and always torn down, so a frozen clock, a vendor
  861:  *   counter or an armed fault cannot leak into the next id â€” and a teardown that REJECTS fails
  862:  *   the test rather than being swallowed, because state that was never released is a defect that
  863:  *   would otherwise land on a different id.
  864:  *
  865:  * THE BODY'S TYPES ARE DERIVED, NOT ACCEPTED. `Sut` and `W` used to be this function's own type
  866:  * parameters, filled in by whatever the suite wrote; now they are `SutOf<R, K>` and `WorldOf<R>`,
  867:  * read off the adapter registered under `R` in `suite-adapters.ts`. There is no type argument left
  868:  * for a suite to lie with.
  869:  *
  870:  * A `const` typed by a call-signature alias, NOT an exported `function` declaration, for the reason
  871:  * given on `defineEvidenceCapture`: a function declaration accepts a merged-in overload and a
  872:  * `const` does not.
  873:  *
  874:  * THE SINGLE-BODY FORM IS TYPED AT THE LOOP TIER, and it is worth saying exactly what that does and
  875:  * does not buy. One body runs at every tier, so it is typed at the tier with the RICHEST
  876:  * capabilities â€” the loop tier, whose clock can be commanded and whose vendor seam can be armed.
  877:  * That keeps every suite in this tree compiling unchanged, and it means the type system does NOT
  878:  * stop a single body from commanding a clock that, above loop, is the passage of time.
  879:  *
  880:  * WHAT DOES STOP IT is the per-tier form: an id whose procedure differs above loop supplies an
  881:  * integration body, and THAT body is typed at `'integration'`, where the two seams are absent from
  882:  * the type. So the protection is opt-in at exactly the ids that need it, which are the ids whose
  883:  * criteria are about real time and real mail. An id that needs it and does not take it fails at run
  884:  * time rather than at compile time, and the fix is to write the body, not to widen the type.
  885:  */
  886: type AtTestFn = {
  887:   <R extends SuiteId, K extends SutKeyOf<R>>(
  888:     atId: string,
  889:     title: string,
  890:     opts: AtTestOptions & SuiteBinding<R, K>,
  891:     body: AtTestBody<R, K, 'loop'> | AtTestBodies<R, K>,
  892:   ): void;
  893: };
  894: 
  895: export const atTest: AtTestFn = <R extends SuiteId, K extends SutKeyOf<R>>(
  896:   atId: string,
  897:   title: string,
  898:   opts: AtTestOptions & SuiteBinding<R, K>,
  899:   body: AtTestBody<R, K, 'loop'> | AtTestBodies<R, K>,
  900: ): void => {
  901:   if (typeof body !== 'function' && (body === null || typeof body !== 'object')) {
  902:     throw new Error(`${atId}: atTest was given no test body`);
  903:   }
  904: 
  905:   /*
  906:    * WHICH BODY RUNS, decided here and once.
  907:    *
  908:    * A bare function is the single-body form: one procedure, every tier, exactly as every suite in
  909:    * this tree is written today. A MAP is the per-tier form, and the tier is resolved from `TIER` â€”
  910:    * the runner's own value, with no default anywhere â€” so the body that registers is the body
  911:    * written for the tier this process is running.
  912:    *
  913:    * A MAP WITH A HOLE IS REFUSED AT REGISTRATION, not skipped. `tierBodyProblem` says why: an id
  914:    * with no body at a tier reports MISSING there, and MISSING is the one state no declaration can
  915:    * describe. Refusing at the call site puts the error where the map is written.
  916:    *
  917:    * WITH `AT_TIER` UNSET there is no tier to choose, so the map's `default` â€” or, failing that, the
  918:    * loop body â€” registers, and `openWorld` refuses with `tier-unset` on its first `open()` exactly
  919:    * as it always has. The alternative would be registering NOTHING, which turns a plainly-diagnosed
  920:    * misuse into an unexplained missing row.
  921:    */
  922:   const resolved: AtTestBody<R, K, 'loop'> = (() => {
  923:     if (typeof body === 'function') return body;
  924:     const problem = tierBodyProblem(body as Record<string, unknown>, atId);
  925:     if (problem) throw new Error(problem);
  926:     const chosen = chooseTierBody(body as Record<string, unknown>, TIER);
  927:     // THE ONE BRIDGE BETWEEN THE TWO TIERS' CONTEXT TYPES, and it is a widening of a NARROWER type.
  928:     // An integration body is written against a context whose clock and vendor seams have FEWER
  929:     // members, so the object built below â€” which has all of them â€” satisfies it; what the cast says
  930:     // is that this function hands one runtime object to bodies written at two different types, and
  931:     // the direction is always narrow-body/wide-object. A body cannot reach a member the object
  932:     // lacks, because no tier's type names one the loop type does not.
  933:     return chosen as AtTestBody<R, K, 'loop'>;
  934:   })();
  935: 
  936:   const parsed = parseAtId(atId);
  937:   const mismatch = requirementMismatch(atId, parsed.requirement, opts.requirement);
  938:   if (mismatch) throw new Error(mismatch);
  939: 
  940:   const previous = registrations.get(atId);
  941:   if (previous) throw new Error(`${atId} is registered twice ("${previous.title}" and "${title}") â€” one id, one test`);
  942:   const registration = { ...parsed, title, surface: opts.surface ?? 'backend' };
  943:   registrations.set(atId, registration);
  944:   emitRuntimeRegistration(registration);
  945: 
  946:   const sutKey = opts.sut;
  947:   const sutMissing =
  948:     opts.sutMissingDetail ?? `REQ-${parsed.requirement}'s implementation is not in the tree â€” harness.sut.${sutKey} is absent`;
  949: 
  950:   // THE TIER'S OWN BUDGET, or vitest's when this id asked for none. `undefined` is what vitest is
  951:   // handed for every id in this tree except the two whose bodies wait out a real access token.
  952:   const timeout = tierTimeout(opts.timeoutMs, TIER);
  953: 
  954:   it(`${atId} â€” ${title}`, async () => {
  955:     expect.hasAssertions();
  956: 
  957:     const worlds: TrackedTeardown[] = [];
  958:     const harnesses: TrackedTeardown[] = [];
  959:     const usage: Usage = { opens: 0, captures: 0 };
  960:     const ctx: InternalContext<SutOf<R, K>, WorldOf<R>, 'loop'> = {
  961:       atId,
  962:       [USAGE]: usage,
  963:       open: async (fixture = `req-${parsed.requirement}/base`, openOpts) => {
  964:         const { opened, harness } = await openWorld({
  965:           atId,
  966:           requirement: parsed.requirement,
  967:           sutKey,
  968:           sutMissing,
  969:           fixture,
  970:           configOverrides: openOpts?.config,
  971:         });
  972:         harnesses.push({ what: `harness for fixture world ${JSON.stringify(fixture)}`, teardown: () => harness.teardown() });
  973:         worlds.push({ what: `fixture world ${JSON.stringify(fixture)}`, teardown: () => opened.w.teardown() });
  974:         usage.opens += 1;
  975:         // THE ONE SURVIVING ASSERTION, and exactly what it still takes on trust.
  976:         //
  977:         // `h.sut[key]` is `unknown` and `h.fixtures.world()` is `WorldSeam`, because `h` is the
  978:         // concrete `AtHarness` the factory is checked to produce and that type deliberately says
  979:         // nothing about any one suite. No run-time value can prove its own static type, so this
  980:         // relabelling cannot be removed â€” only moved to where the claim is smallest.
  981:         //
  982:         // What changed is WHOSE claim it is. It used to be the suite's: `bindSuite<X, Y>` named two
  983:         // types and this line stamped them onto whatever the harness returned, so any pair of names
  984:         // was accepted. Now both come from `suite-adapters.ts`, which reads them off the adapter
  985:         // module that the `requirement` literal proves is the module `loadAdapter()` imports â€”
  986:         // checked at the map entry, and again at run time in `index.ts`.
  987:         //
  988:         // So what remains unchecked here is narrow and nameable: that the object `createHarness()`
  989:         // built at run time really is the one that module's `createFixtureAdapter()` returned. That
  990:         // holds unless somebody casts inside `index.ts` or mutates the adapter after it is built â€”
  991:         // both trusted-author escapes this item does not claim to close.
  992:         return opened as OpenWorld<R, K, 'loop'>;
  993:       },
  994:       capture: async (evidence) => {
  995:         const value = await evidence.consume(ctx);
  996:         usage.captures += 1;
  997:         return value;
  998:       },
  999:     };
 1000: 
 1001:     await runTrackedTest(atId, () => executeRegisteredBody(atId, resolved, ctx, usage), worlds, harnesses);
 1002:   }, timeout);
 1003: };
