   80: 
   81: - **`/work` is the only verb.** Claiming, building, merging and closing are PHASES inside it,
   82:   never separate verbs â€” a second way to close work is how unreviewed work lands while the way of
   83:   work appears followed.
   84: - **`/work` IS INVOKED FRESH AT EVERY ITEM START â€” never executed from memory of a prior
   85:   reading (MUST-FOLLOW, founder ruling 2026-08-11).** A skill's text freezes in context at the
   86:   moment it is invoked, and merged items change the skill mid-day; a coordinator that recites the
   87:   procedure runs the version in its head, not the one on main. This is a measured failure, not a
   88:   hypothesis: AI4DEV-80 (attribution by spawn tree) was started without the database-slot step
   89:   that AI4DEV-79 (database slot pool) had merged into the skill hours earlier, and the founder
   90:   caught it from the outside by asking why no slot messages appeared.
   91: - **ATTRIBUTION IS DERIVED FROM THE BRANCH, NEVER DECLARED (MUST-FOLLOW).** cwd â†’ git worktree â†’
   92:   branch â†’ exactly one item id â†’ walk `parent` upward for the chain. The branch is primary
   93:   because it is coupled to closure: its pull request closes that item, so a wrong branch shows up
   94:   as a wrong closure on the board instead of hiding. Every attribution failure this project has
   95:   had was a declared fact drifting from a real one with nothing able to notice.
   96: - **The held item is a CROSS-CHECK, never an answer.** `/work` records what the session holds and
   97:   the stamp compares it to the branch every prompt; it can raise CONFLICT or fill a gap the
   98:   branch left empty, but it can NEVER override the branch. A stale held item makes the stamp
   99:   louder, never wronger.
  100: - **Attribution degrades, never blocks.** Linear unreachable, chain ambiguous, root missing: the
  101:   stamp says so and the work proceeds. The ONE thing it blocks is closing a requirement, because
  102:   that is where a wrong answer becomes permanent.
  103: - **OPEN EVERY REPLY WITH THIS TURN'S PICTURE, THEN THE HOOK'S FACTS (MUST-FOLLOW; founder
  104:   2026-08-09, superseding the verbatim-echo rule of 2026-08-07 â€” "what I want from your echo is
  105:   todo the mangling to take the hook facts and take into account the user prompt").** Both lines
  106:   are PLAIN TEXT â€” never wrapped in backticks or a fenced code block (founder 2026-08-11: the
  107:   content was meant, not a code wrap). **The header leads the FIRST text the turn emits**
  108:   (founder correction 2026-08-11: a status line before tool calls arrived bare, and the founder
  109:   read "response first, stamp after") â€” interim narration before or between tool calls runs
  110:   UNDER the header, never above it; the turn's later messages do not repeat it. Two lines:
  111:   - `[<session tag>] TURN  <attribution> - <what this response is about>` â€” **every line of the
  112:     reply header carries the session prefix, the `TURN` line included** (founder 2026-08-11).
  113:     The tag is the one the hook prints; when the hook emitted none, the prefix is omitted rather
  114:     than invented. The reason is the reason the prefix exists at all: two sessions' output can
  115:     sit side by side in one view, and a header line without its speaker is an orphan â€” the
  116:     synthesis line most of all, because it is the one the founder reads first.
  117:     The `TURN` line is the model's OPEN SYNTHESIS of the
  118:     hook facts and the current prompt, so the founder sees what this actual turn is about,
  119:     including attribution AFTER any direction change the prompt just made. A `TURN` line names a
  120:     board item only when the turn genuinely works that item; otherwise a NAMED floating root that
  121:     states its subject â€” `~exploration (<topic>)` for untracked investigation, or
  122:     `~bringup (<topic>)` for foundation work that has no PM requirement above it (founder
  123:     2026-08-11). **A bare `~floating` is banned** (founder, twice: 2026-08-09 "this should be at
  124:     least exploration or something more informing", and 2026-08-11 "I don't like floating") â€” an
  125:     attribution the reader cannot act on is the same defect as a bare internal code. So a TURN
  126:     line carries one of exactly these: a chain of item ids with short titles, `~exploration
  127:     (<what, in words>)`, or `~bringup (<what, in words>)`. The rule is that the label NAMES its
  128:     subject, not that only one root exists.
  129:     **ATTRIBUTION MEANS THE WHOLE CHAIN, root first, every id with its short title** (founder
  130:     correction 2026-08-09: *"Note that the stamp doesn't show the chain"*). Print
  131:     `AI4PM-19 (auth and org membership) > AI4DEV-50 (auth root) > AI4DEV-59 (GitHub sign-in)`,
  132:     never the leaf alone. The leaf alone hides which requirement the turn serves, which is the
  133:     one thing the chain exists to show.
  134:   - **The `HOOK` block REPRODUCES the hook's own lines. The only permitted edit is dropping the
  135:     machine-readable `<ai4good-attribution .../>` tag** (founder corrections 2026-08-09, twice:
  136:     the chain went missing, then the branch went missing). Every other line stands as emitted â€”
  137:     each actor's `WORKING ON` line with its full chain, each `IN` line with its folder **and its
  138:     branch**, and every qualifier. Compression is what kept losing evidence, so there is no
  139:     compression: **line 1 is a conclusion and line 2 is what it was derived from, and a
  140:     conclusion without its derivation is exactly the failure the stamp exists to prevent.** If
  141:     the hook emitted nothing, say so; if a value is known wrong, print it as emitted and dispute
  142:     it on the next line.
  143: 
  144:   The split is the safety: judgment and evidence stay distinguishable, and a DIVERGENCE between
  145:   the two lines is itself the signal that direction changed â€” the same turn must then update the
  146:   held facts (`Set-HeldItem`, or clear) so the next prompt's hook agrees again. The stamp itself
  147:   stays script-derived and judgment-free; only the reply's opening line synthesizes.
  148:   **Anything relayed from another actor carries the arrival time and that actor's role**
  149:   (`09:41 Â· conductor â€” plan â†’ gate 1`), so a claim is never read as the coordinator's own.
  150: - **PRINT THE TRANSITION LINE THE MOMENT THE BRANCH CHANGES (MUST-FOLLOW).** The stamp describes
  151:   the turn as it began, so a turn that changes branch must say so in the transcript.
  152: - **The agent never moves itself between folders.** A session works where it was launched, on one
  153:   branch, for the whole item. It may create a worktree and ask the founder to open a session
  154:   there; it never teleports. Serial work needs no worktree at all â€” switch the branch you are on.
  155: - **Every id printed carries a very short title in parentheses** â€” `AI4DEV-19 (H3 sentinels)`,
  156:   two to five words, a recall hint, never Linear's full sentence.
  157: - **NEVER open a dev item for mechanics â€” it rides along** (founder 2026-08-02). Machinery changed
  158:   while working an item goes in that item's branch and PR, listed under "rides along". Independent
  159:   work that could stand alone is **filed, not built**.
  160: - **One session, one item.** No nesting: `/work` at something else while one is open offers
  161:   finish, park, or file.
  162: - **Requirements close only through the evidence gate** â€” exhaustive leaf snapshot at a named
  163:   commit, attribution resolved, suite green at integration tier with named checks and timestamps,
  164:   recorded founder attestation, explicit waiver path. `/work` proposes; it never closes one alone.
  165: - **Blocked** is a label + comment, never a status change.
  166: - **Doc changes** run through `/doc-sync fold` (git â†’ Linear only; meaning never changes in
  167:   Linear). PRD text is edited ONLY in `loop/out/pure-s*.md`.
  168: - **Commits** cite the item they belong to.
  169: 
  170: **Anti-patterns:**
  171: - Hand-editing board status in the Linear UI, or setting an item Done by hand â€” the merge does it.
  172: - Closing a requirement with open leaves, or on cached/stale attribution.
  173: - A second way to close work, however convenient it looks.
  174: - Committing foundation work straight to `main`: branch, pull request, merge, like everything else.
  175: - Ceremony out of proportion to the work â€” that is how a process stops being followed at all.
  176: - Editing `prd-mvp.md`, an isolate, or Linear item text directly to change meaning.
  177: 
  178: **Foundation work (W0 bring-up)** is dev-board work with no requirement above it. It is not a
  179: product requirement and has no evidence gate, but it runs through `/work` exactly like everything
  180: else and closes on a merged pull request. Its chain simply ends on a bring-up root
  181: (`AI4DEV-3 (AT harness)`, `AI4DEV-4 (the work skill)`) instead of on a requirement â€” which is
  182: what marks it as infrastructure. Nothing is declared to say so, and nothing can be faked.
