  184: ---
  185: ## Communication: simple English, never shorthand (founder instruction, stated repeatedly â€” 2026-07-16, 2026-07-18 and 2026-07-28)
  186: 
  187: **ALWAYS USE ASD-STE100 SIMPLIFIED TECHNICAL ENGLISH (MUST-FOLLOW, founder 2026-08-09).** This
  188: is the aerospace controlled-language standard, and it applies to everything written for a
  189: person: replies, reports, plans, board items, commit and pull-request bodies, and the process
  190: files. What it means in practice:
  191: - **One word, one meaning. One meaning, one word.** Choose a term and keep it for the life of
  192:   the document. Do not reach for a synonym to avoid repetition â€” in a controlled language,
  193:   repetition is the feature.
  194: - **Short sentences.** Twenty words maximum in a procedure, twenty-five in a description. One
  195:   instruction per sentence.
  196: - **Active voice, present tense**, with the actor named: "the conductor spawns the runner", not
  197:   "the runner is spawned".
  198: - **No noun clusters longer than three words**, and no jargon, idiom, or metaphor where a plain
  199:   word exists.
  200: - **Say the condition first**, then the action: "If the gate is empty, report it as empty."
  201: - Keep paragraphs to about six sentences.
  202: 
  203: This tightens the rules below; it never loosens them. Where full compliance would make a
  204: technical fact wrong or unsayable, keep the fact and say plainly that you did.
  205: 
  206: When reporting to or planning with the founder, write in plain sentences. Do not lean on
  207: invented labels or compressed codes â€” "P3", "W1", "T4", "d82", "r2 fold" mean nothing on
  208: their own. Rules:
  209: - Never use an internal label without saying in words what the thing is, in the same
  210:   sentence. "Protect the main branch on GitHub" â€” not "P3". "The decision that split
  211:   Linear into a PM tree and a dev tree" â€” not just "d82".
  212: - Requirement and decision numbers are fine as references, but always next to a plain
  213:   description, never instead of one.
  214: - **NEVER PRINT A BARE ITEM NUMBER â€” always `id (very short title)` in PARENTHESES
  215:   (MUST-FOLLOW, founder instruction 2026-08-01: *"always have in wrapped in () a very short
  216:   text title for an item so no more AIPM-12 or AIDEV-14 without a quick text recall for me"*).**
  217:   Write `AI4DEV-19 (H3 sentinels)` â€” never `AI4DEV-19` alone, and not the dash-and-full-title
  218:   shape this rule had earlier the same day.
  219:   - **Very short means a RECALL HINT, not the real title:** two to five words. Linear titles
  220:     are often a whole sentence; shorten them, never paste them. `AI4DEV-35 (short titles in
  221:     parens)`, not `AI4DEV-35 (Every item id printed to the founder carries a very short title
  222:     in parentheses)`.
  223:   - **STRIP INTERNAL CODES â€” the label must say what the thing IS** (founder 2026-08-03: *"I
  224:     can't understand the H5 or its equiv means nothing to me. Human title near the dev label
  225:     should be more informative."*). Many board titles
  226:     lead with a code â€” `H5 â€” `, `REQ-0NN â€” `, `Batch 3 â€” `. Truncating from the front keeps the
  227:     code and throws away the meaning, which is the exact opposite of the point. Drop the code
  228:     and describe the thing:
  229:     - `AI4DEV-21 (fake Stripe, GitHub, Anthropic)` â€” not `(H5 vendor stand-ins)`
  230:     - `AI4DEV-20 (judging AI output meaning)` â€” not `(H4 semantic-oracle)`
  231:     - `AI4DEV-22 (first requirement green end to end)` â€” not `(H7 proving ground)`
  232:     A label a stranger could not act on is not a label; it is the id twice.
  233:   - Applies everywhere an id reaches the founder: replies, status reports, board listings,
  234:     suggestions of what to work on next, prose in commit and PR bodies, and the WORKING-ON
  235:     disclaimer â€” where the stamp hook formats `id (label)` from a cached short label, because
  236:     the hook runs before every prompt and must never call Linear.
  237:   - If the label is genuinely unknown, look it up; a bare id is acceptable only when the lookup
  238:     itself failed, and then say so.
  239: - **NEVER NAME ANOTHER ITEM'S ID IN A PULL REQUEST TITLE OR BODY (MUST-FOLLOW).** The id itself
  240:   is the trigger, not the verb beside it. Linear links a pull request to every id in its text,
  241:   and **the link alone moves that item** â€” a finished item was dragged back to In Progress
  242:   twenty-four minutes after its own merge had correctly closed it, by a body that said
  243:   *"ref AI4DEV-43"*. A closing verb (**close / fix / resolve / complete / implement** and their
  244:   inflections) additionally *closes* the item â€” louder, but the same defect. A careful
  245:   post-mortem once closed the item it learned from **mid-work**, because the sentence read
  246:   *"the instruction that fixed AI4DEV-31's chain"*. The hazard punishes good writing: the more
  247:   thorough the explanation, the more items it cites, and the item most likely to be cited is the
  248:   one someone is actively working.
  249:   - **There is NO safe reference word.** `ref`, `references`, `part of`, `related to`,
  250:     `contributes to` and `towards` all link the item and all move it. Earlier guidance here
  251:     recommended them, and that advice caused the very defect it was meant to prevent.
  252:   - Name other items **in words**: *"the item that landed the relay"*, *"the requirement above
  253:     this one"*. TWO exceptions only: the branch's own item â€” that link is what closes it â€” and,
  254:     since the batching mode (founder 2026-08-11), **a batch partner's closes-line**: one line of
  255:     the exact shape `Closes AI4DEV-nn`, alone on its line, at most one per pull request,
  256:     declared in the merge ruling. That line closes the partner on merge deliberately; it is the
  257:     integration used on purpose, once, and the guard verifies its shape.
  258:   - CI enforces this on every pull request: **any** id the branch does not own fails the build,
  259:     except the one sanctioned closes-line above.
  260:     The rule is written here because the guard should never be the first place you learn it.
  261: - A status update should read like an explanation to a smart teammate who has NOT been
  262:   following the internal naming â€” because that is exactly the situation.
  263: - Lists of steps get described by what the step does, not by its stage code.
  264: 
  265: ### Writing about what the founder said (MUST-FOLLOW)
  266: 
  267: A provenance audit of these files found polished paraphrases presented as quotations and dates
  268: with no message behind them. Every one looked like evidence.
  269: 
  270: - **Quote exactly, or do not use quote marks.** Typos, missing words and all. Tidying someone's
  271:   words and then presenting them as a quotation is fabrication at small scale.
  272: - **Cite a date only where a message exists on that day**, in the founder's local time.
  273:   Transcripts are UTC and the founder is UTC+3, so a 21:00 UTC message belongs to the next day.
  274: - **Never convert "the founder asked" into "the founder ruled."** A question that prompted a good
  275:   rule is provenance for the question, not for the rule.
  276: - **A rule that LOOSENS the process needs a real, explicit founder ruling.** Tightening may be
  277:   proposed; loosening may never be inferred. (This rule worked the first time it was tested: a
  278:   draft deleted the reflection step with nothing behind it, review restored it, and the founder
  279:   then ruled it out explicitly â€” *"Reflection should be out"*, 2026-08-06.)
  280: 
  281: ---
  282: ## Project-Specific Guidelines
  283: 
  284: - **Use the Lovable MCP for non-trivial UI work.** For UI changes beyond simple tweaks, drive them through the Lovable MCP â€” Lovable is the bot operating its own MCP and has more intimate, UI/UX-optimized capabilities. Reserve direct edits for simple UI changes.
  285: - **UI never touches the DB directly.** UI code must always go through an edge function â€” never call the database directly from UI code.
  286: 
  287: **These guidelines are working if:** fewer unnecessary changes in diffs, fewer rewrites due to overcomplication, and clarifying questions come before implementation rather than after mistakes.
